.docname {Project structure}
.include {docs}

vCortex is one repository with two programs: a **backend** that builds the twin, and a
**frontend** that shows it in the browser. This page walks through the folders, top to bottom.

```text
vCortex/
├── backend/            the C++ program that turns a map into a twin
│   ├── config/         start values for the model
│   ├── include/vcortex/ what each part offers (headers)
│   ├── src/            how each part does it (same folders as include/)
│   ├── schema/         the shape of the twin file
│   └── tests/          checks for the hardest steps
├── frontend/           the web page with the two views
│   ├── public/         the logo
│   └── src/            the page itself
├── docs/               this documentation
├── data/               saved maps for offline mode (kept out of git)
├── .github/workflows/  automatic jobs on GitHub
└── compose.yaml        starts backend and frontend together
```

## The top level

| Path | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `backend/` | The program that receives the map, runs the model and writes the outputs. Written in C++17, built with CMake, with no ROS inside. |
| `frontend/` | The web page: raw dots on the left, the twin on the right. Written in Vue 3 and TypeScript, drawn in 3D with TresJS (a Vue wrapper around three.js). |
| `docs/` | This documentation, written in Quarkdown. |
| `data/` | Saved maps to open without a robot. Only the empty folder is in git. |
| `.github/workflows/` | The job that builds this documentation and publishes it on GitHub Pages. |
| `compose.yaml` | Runs both programs in containers with one command (see [Usage](usage.qd)). |
| `.env.example` | A template for local secrets, such as the token a robot must show to send its map. |

## The backend

The backend is split into layers. Each layer may use the ones below it, never the ones above. So
the model knows nothing about the network, files or threads, and can be tested on its own.

.mermaid caption:{Backend layers: each one uses only the layers below it.}
    flowchart TD
        IO["io: server, worker, files"] --> C["connectors: output formats"]
        IO --> P["protocol: messages on the wire"]
        C --> M["model: from dots to a twin"]
        IO --> M
        M --> G["geometry: small shared helpers"]
        M --> D["domain: the twin's parts"]
        G --> D
        C --> D
        P --> D

Every layer is a folder under `include/vcortex/` (what it offers) and the same folder under
`src/` (how it does it).

| Folder | Layer | What it holds |
|---|---|---|
| `domain/` | Domain | The parts of the [domain](domain.qd) as plain data: building, level, wall, room, object; a lidar point; the map and path received so far; the model settings. |
| `geometry/` | Helpers | Small math shared by the model: bounding boxes, grid cells, distances to a wall. |
| `model/` | Model | The core: everything that turns dots into a twin. See below. |
| `connectors/` | Output | One file per output format: twin JSON, DXF floor plan, PLY cloud. |
| `protocol/` | Messages | The byte layout of what a robot sends in, and of the dots sent to the browser. |
| `io/` | Outside world | The web server, the thread that reruns the model every few seconds, the map store, reading settings and saved maps. |
| `src/main.cpp` | Entry point | Reads the command line and starts the server. |

### Inside `model/`

The model follows the steps of the [Algorithm](algorithm.qd), and its folders follow them too.

| Path | What it does |
|---|---|
| `clean` | Drops stray dots. |
| `floor/floor` | Finds the floor plane, so heights are measured from it. |
| `floor/free_space` | Finds the floor the robot could reach, and splits it into rooms at doors. |
| `align` | Finds how the map is turned, so walls can run along the axes. |
| `grid` | A top view of the dots, counted in small squares. |
| `wall/outline` | Walks out from each room to find the inner face of its walls. |
| `wall/walls` | Turns those faces into walls with a thickness. |
| `rooms` | Builds each room and the walls around it. |
| `object/` | Groups the leftover dots into objects and fits a box around each. |
| `median` | A small helper: the middle value of a list. |
| `model` | Runs all the steps in order and reports what each one found. |

### The other backend folders

| Path | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `config/model.json` | The start values of the model: wall height, grid size, distances. You can change them while it runs. |
| `schema/twin.schema.json` | The exact shape of the twin JSON file. The frontend builds its types from it. |
| `tests/` | Checks for the hardest model steps: free floor, wall outlines, walls, object boxes. Rooms are drawn as text, so a test reads like a small map. |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | Builds two libraries and the program: `vcortex_core` (domain to protocol), `vcortex_io` (the outside world) and `vcortex`. |
| `Dockerfile` | Builds the backend image: one program, no shell, run as a normal user. |

## The frontend

| Path | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `src/views/` | The one page of the app. |
| `src/components/` | The pieces of the page: header, raw view, twin view, status bar, camera buttons. |
| `src/components/renderers/` | One 3D scene per output format: twin, floor plan, cleaned cloud. |
| `src/scene/` | Building the 3D shapes: dots, walls, boxes, camera. |
| `src/services/` | Talking to the backend: the settings API and the two live connections (raw dots and twin). |
| `src/types/` | The twin types, generated from the backend's schema. |
| `Caddyfile` | The small web server in the container: it serves the page and passes `/api` and `/ws` on to the backend. |
| `Dockerfile` | Builds the frontend image. |
